  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At Home in Renaissance Bruges is showing an early but credible scholarly footprint, with 3 OpenAlex citations and 4,918 recorded downloads, alongside open access availability through repository and platform records. Its strongest signal is sustained use: readers are accessing the book at a level that suggests continuing interest in its reconstruction of domestic life in sixteenth-century Bruges. The record also shows funded support from KU Leuven, the Fondation Universitaire de Belgique, and the University of Antwerp, and a clear thematic link to Sustainable Cities and Communities.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Julie De Groot\u2019s At Home in Renaissance Bruges examines how citizens created domestic life in sixteenth-century Bruges, using probate inventories to reconstruct household interiors and domestic culture from written sources. The book sits at the intersection of Renaissance studies, art history, and social history, and the available record indicates that it has already begun to circulate beyond its immediate subject area.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest impact signal is usage. The book has been downloaded 4,918 times through OAPEN, which points to substantial reader interest and suggests that the work is being consulted as a research resource. This is complemented by open access availability, which likely supports wider discovery and uptake. Citation evidence is still early, with 3 OpenAlex citations recorded, so the scholarly conversation around the book is developing rather than mature.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape shows that the book is already being drawn into work on adjacent historical questions, including an article in Revista de Historia Moderna and a dissertation in the University of Groningen research database. That pattern suggests disciplinary reach beyond Bruges itself, with later users engaging the book as a source for broader questions in early modern history and material culture. The record is still sparse, but the citations that are present indicate that the book is entering scholarly circulation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The funding record and the SDG alignment add further context. Support from KU Leuven, the Fondation Universitaire de Belgique, and the University of Antwerp underlines institutional backing for the research, while the Sustainable Cities and Communities mapping reflects the book\u2019s relevance to how people shape and inhabit urban environments. Overall, the evidence points to a work that is already being read and cited, with the strongest signal coming from use and access rather than from a large citation base.</p>",
